COURSES = {}

COURSES['leadership-essentials'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('Leadership is a behaviour',
         ['Leadership is what you do, not your title', 'Anyone can lead in a moment', 'Small behaviours add up'],
         "Welcome to Leadership Essentials. Leadership is often linked to a job title, but it is really a set of behaviours. Anyone can lead in a moment, by taking ownership of a problem, helping a colleague, or making a clear suggestion. That means you can start practising now, whatever your role. In this lecture we cover taking ownership without taking over, communicating a clear next step, and building trust through small kept promises. These are simple ideas, but they separate people others want to follow from those they simply work for."),
        ('Ownership without taking over',
         ['Own the outcome, share the work', 'Ask before you step in', 'Let others do it their way'],
         "Taking ownership means caring about the result and following it through. It does not mean doing everything yourself. A common mistake is stepping in and taking over when someone is struggling, which solves today's problem but weakens the person. Ask before you step in, for example, would it help if I took a look. And let others do it their way, as long as the outcome is met. Good leaders are comfortable with something done differently from how they would have done it. Ownership is about responsibility, not about control."),
        ('Clarity beats charisma',
         ['A clear request has an outcome, an owner and a date', 'Say less, and make it specific', 'Check that it was understood'],
         "Many people believe leaders need charisma. In daily work, clarity matters far more. A clear request has three parts: the outcome you want, who owns it, and by when. Vague requests such as, can someone look at this, usually result in nothing, or in two people doing the same thing. Say less, and make it specific. Then check that it was understood, by asking the person to say in their own words what they will do. This takes thirty seconds and prevents days of confusion. Clear communication is a gift to the people around you."),
        ('Communicating a clear next step',
         ['End every meeting with actions', 'Write them down and share them', 'Follow up on the date you agreed'],
         "One of the simplest leadership habits is to end every conversation with a clear next step. At the end of a meeting, summarise what was decided, who will do what, and by when. Write it down and share it the same day. Then follow up on the date you agreed, even if only with a quick message. This habit builds a reputation for reliability. People learn that when you are involved, things move. Many meetings fail not because of the discussion, but because nobody was sure what happened afterwards."),
        ('Earning trust',
         ['Trust grows from small kept promises', 'Do what you said you would do', 'If you cannot, say so early'],
         "Trust is not built through big speeches. It grows from small kept promises. If you say you will send something by Friday, send it by Friday. If you say you will raise a concern with the manager, do it, and report back. When you cannot keep a promise, say so early and explain, instead of going quiet. People forgive missed deadlines far more easily than silence. Over time, a record of small kept promises becomes a reputation, and a reputation for being reliable is the foundation of leadership."),
        ('Giving credit and taking responsibility',
         ['Share credit generously', 'Take responsibility when things go wrong', 'Learn out loud'],
         "Two habits mark out good leaders. They share credit generously, naming the people who did the work, in front of others. And they take responsibility when things go wrong, saying what they will do differently instead of pointing at others. Learning out loud, such as saying, I made a call that did not work, here is what I learned, shows others that mistakes are survivable. It makes it easier for everyone to be honest. Teams where credit is shared and responsibility is owned tend to be more open and more resilient."),
        ('Listening as a leadership skill',
         ['Give full attention', 'Ask a follow-up question', 'Summarise before you respond'],
         "Listening is one of the most underrated leadership skills. Give people your full attention, which means putting your phone away. Ask a follow-up question that shows you want to understand more. And summarise what you heard before you respond. This simple habit slows the conversation down just enough to avoid misunderstandings. People who feel listened to are more willing to share problems early, and to support decisions even when they did not get their way. Leaders who listen well usually know what is actually going on."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Use the outcome, owner, date format this week', 'Keep one visible promise', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, use the outcome, owner and date format for every request you make this week. Choose one promise to keep visibly, and tell someone about it. Complete the workbook for this course, which includes a clear request template and a promise tracker. Then take the final quiz. When you reach the end of the last lesson you will receive your certificate and a personal review of how you worked through the course. Leadership starts with small, consistent behaviours, so start with one today."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('Ownership', 'Taking responsibility for an outcome and following it through.'),
        ('Clear request', 'A request that names the outcome, the owner and the date.'),
        ('Kept promise', 'A small commitment completed as agreed, or renegotiated early.'),
        ('Credit', 'Public recognition of the people who did the work.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'Where do you step in and take over when you could ask first?',
        'What request did you make recently that was not clear, and what happened?',
        'Which promise have you made that you have not yet kept?',
    ],
    exercise=('Clear request practice', [
        'Choose three requests you will make this week.',
        'Write each with an outcome, an owner and a date.',
        'Ask each person to repeat back what they will do.',
        'Note how many were completed on time.',
    ]),
)

COURSES['team-foundations'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('What great teams share',
         ['Psychological safety', 'Clear goals and roles', 'Reliable follow-through'],
         "Welcome to Team Foundations. Teams that work well tend to share three things. People feel safe to speak up, which is called psychological safety. The goals and roles are clear, so everybody knows what they are doing and why. And there is reliable follow-through, so when something is agreed, it happens. This lecture shows what each of those looks like on an ordinary day, and how any team member, not only the manager, can contribute to them. We will cover meetings, speaking up, and how to raise concerns early and kindly."),
        ('What healthy teams look like day to day',
         ['People ask questions openly', 'Disagreements are about the work', 'Help is offered and accepted'],
         "In a healthy team, people ask questions without fear of looking foolish. Disagreements happen, but they stay about the work, not about people. Help is offered and accepted easily. Successes are shared and mistakes are discussed openly. None of this requires everyone to be best friends. It requires respect, and some habits that make respect visible. You can start those habits yourself. Thanking a colleague, asking a genuine question, or admitting a mistake all make it easier for others to do the same. Teams are shaped by what each person does."),
        ('Meetings that matter',
         ['Prepare before you arrive', 'Contribute, and make space for others', 'Summarise and follow up'],
         "Meetings take up a lot of time, so it is worth making them useful. Prepare before you arrive, by reading the agenda and thinking about what you can add. During the meeting, contribute, and also make space for others, especially quieter colleagues. Ask, what do you think. At the end, summarise what was decided and who will do what. After the meeting, follow up in writing. Anyone can improve a meeting this way, whether or not they are leading it. A short, clear summary is often the most valued contribution in the room."),
        ('Being a good contributor',
         ['Be on time and present', 'Give ideas, not only problems', 'Build on what others say'],
         "Good contributors are reliable and constructive. Be on time and be present, without checking your phone. When you raise a problem, try to bring an idea along with it. Build on what others say, using phrases like, yes, and we could also. This keeps momentum, and shows that you are listening. Avoid dismissing ideas too quickly, since the first version of an idea is rarely the best. Over time, people come to see you as someone who makes the group better, and that is a powerful reputation in any team."),
        ('Speaking up',
         ['Decide what you want to say', 'Be specific and calm', 'Raise it early'],
         "Speaking up can feel risky, but silence has costs too. Before you speak, decide the one thing you want to say. Be specific, for example, I am worried the deadline does not allow time for testing. Keep your tone calm, and focus on the issue, not on a person. Raise it early, when there is still time to change course. If you are unsure, frame it as a question, such as, have we thought about how this affects the customer. Speaking up well is a skill, and it gets easier each time you do it."),
        ('Responding when someone speaks up',
         ['Say thank you', 'Ask a question before you answer', 'Show what you will do with it'],
         "The other side of speaking up is how you respond when a colleague raises a concern. Start with thank you. Ask a question to understand it before you answer, such as, can you tell me more about what you are seeing. And show what you will do with the information, even if the answer is that you need time to think. If people raise concerns and nothing happens, they stop. If something happens, even something small, they keep going. How the team responds to the first concern decides whether there is a second."),
        ('Handling disagreement kindly',
         ['Assume good intent', 'Separate the idea from the person', 'Look for what you agree on first'],
         "Disagreement is healthy when handled well. Assume good intent, which means thinking the other person is trying to help, until you see otherwise. Separate the idea from the person, by saying, I see it differently, rather than, you are wrong. Look for what you agree on first, because that gives you a base to work from. Ask what would change your mind, and what would change theirs. When a disagreement cannot be resolved, agree who decides, and then support the decision. Respect in disagreement builds trust faster than avoiding it."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Add one thing to your next meeting', 'Raise one small concern kindly', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, choose one thing that you will add to your next meeting, such as a summary of actions. Raise one small concern kindly, using the approach from this lecture. Complete the workbook for this course, which has a meeting checklist and a speaking up planner. Then take the final quiz. When you reach the end of the last lesson you will receive your certificate and a personal review of how you worked through the course. Teams improve one person's habit at a time, and you can start today."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('Psychological safety', 'The belief that you can speak up without fear of embarrassment or punishment.'),
        ('Follow-through', 'Doing what was agreed, by the date it was agreed.'),
        ('Constructive', 'Helping move something forward, for example by offering an idea alongside a problem.'),
        ('Good intent', 'The assumption that a colleague is trying to help even when you disagree.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'Which of the three shared traits of great teams is strongest in your team, and which is weakest?',
        'What stopped you the last time you wanted to speak up?',
        'How did your team respond the last time someone raised a concern?',
    ],
    exercise=('Meeting contribution plan', [
        'Read the agenda and write one point you can add.',
        'Choose one quiet colleague and plan to ask for their view.',
        'Write a two-line summary of decisions at the end.',
        'Send it within a day.',
    ]),
)

COURSES['high-performing-teams'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('What holds teams back',
         ['Performance has causes you can find', 'Most issues are trust, clarity or energy', 'Diagnose before you fix'],
         "Welcome to Building High-Performing Teams. When a team underperforms, leaders often reach for a fix straight away, such as a new process or a team day out. But the cause may be something else. This lecture is about diagnosing first. Most team problems fall under trust, clarity or energy. Once you know which, the right action becomes obvious. We will cover how to run a quick team health check, how to design rituals that stick, and how to align several teams around shared goals, so that groups of teams work together instead of against each other."),
        ('Diagnosing a team',
         ['Trust: do people speak openly', 'Clarity: do they know goals and roles', 'Energy: do they feel able to do good work'],
         "A quick diagnosis looks at three areas. Trust, which asks whether people speak openly and rely on each other. Clarity, which asks whether people know the goals, the roles and what good looks like. And energy, which asks whether people feel able to do good work, or whether they are stretched and tired. Ask short questions on each, using a simple scale. Do it anonymously, so that people are honest. The scores will not give you the full story, but they show where to look and where to start the conversation."),
        ('Sharing results without blame',
         ['Share the scores openly', 'Ask what stands out to the team', 'Choose one thing to work on'],
         "Sharing the results is where many leaders go wrong. If the data feels like a judgement, people become defensive. Present the scores as information about the team, not about individuals. Ask the team what stands out to them, and what they would add. Listen more than you talk. Then choose one thing to work on, not five. A single, shared priority is more likely to move than a long list. Agree who will lead it and when you will review progress. The process of looking together often improves trust by itself."),
        ('Rituals that stick',
         ['Small, regular and visible', 'Tied to something that already happens', 'Owned by the team, not only the leader'],
         "Most team agreements fade by Wednesday. Rituals help them last. A ritual is a small, regular action that the team does together, such as a short start of week check in, a Friday round of thanks, or a monthly look at what to stop doing. The best rituals are small, regular and visible. Tie them to something that already happens, such as an existing meeting. And make them owned by the team, so that they continue when the leader is away. Start with one ritual, and keep it going for two months before adding another."),
        ('Designing a good ritual',
         ['Decide the purpose in one sentence', 'Keep it under fifteen minutes', 'Review whether it is still useful'],
         "To design a ritual, begin with the purpose in one sentence. For example, to make sure everyone knows the priorities for the week. Keep it under fifteen minutes, since long rituals get skipped. Rotate who leads it, so that the ownership is shared. And review every couple of months whether it is still useful. Drop it if not, because a ritual that has lost its point is worse than none. The aim is a small number of habits that people value, not a calendar full of obligations."),
        ('Teams of teams',
         ['Shared goals across teams', 'Clear interfaces between teams', 'Leaders who connect rather than control'],
         "When several teams need to work together, new problems appear. Each team may optimise for its own goals, while the overall result suffers. To scale well, give the teams shared goals that they can all see. Make the interfaces between teams clear, meaning what each team provides to the others, and how to ask for it. And lead by connecting rather than controlling. Bring team leads together regularly, so that they hear each other's challenges. When teams understand each other's work, handovers improve, and conflict falls."),
        ('Handling dependencies and handovers',
         ['Name dependencies early', 'Agree what a good handover looks like', 'Fix problems at the source'],
         "Many delays in larger organisations come from dependencies between teams. Name them early, in planning, rather than discovering them late. Agree what a good handover looks like, including what information is passed on and in what form. When something goes wrong, look for the cause at the source, rather than blaming the team where the problem showed up. A short regular meeting between teams that rely on each other can prevent months of friction. These are practical steps, but they make teams of teams feel like one organisation."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Run a three-question health check', 'Start one ritual for two months', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, run the three question health check with your team this month, and share the results openly. Choose one ritual to start, and commit to it for two months. Complete the workbook for this course, which has the health check form and a ritual design page. Then take the final quiz. When you reach the end of the last lesson you will receive your certificate and a personal review of how you worked through the course. High performance is built from steady habits, so begin with one and build from there."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('Team health check', 'A short anonymous survey of trust, clarity and energy.'),
        ('Ritual', 'A small, regular, visible team action that keeps an agreement alive.'),
        ('Dependency', 'A piece of work that one team needs from another before it can proceed.'),
        ('Interface', 'The agreed way one team requests and receives work from another.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'Is your team\'s main constraint trust, clarity or energy, and how do you know?',
        'Which team ritual is already working, and what makes it stick?',
        'Which other team do you depend on most, and how clear is the handover?',
    ],
    exercise=('Three-question health check', [
        'Write three questions on trust, clarity and energy, scored one to five.',
        'Collect the answers anonymously.',
        'Share the results and ask what stands out.',
        'Agree one action and an owner.',
    ]),
)

COURSES['wellbeing-strategy-for-leaders'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('Beyond the wellness app',
         ['Perks rarely change outcomes', 'Strain usually comes from work design', 'Strategy starts with workload'],
         "Welcome to Well-being Strategy for Leaders. Many organisations respond to well-being concerns with perks, such as an app, a yoga class or a fruit bowl. These can be pleasant, but they rarely change how people feel about work. The biggest drivers of strain usually sit in how work is designed, including workload, control, clarity and relationships. This lecture covers how to link well-being to workload and the design of work, how to build a plan with real measures, and how to make support part of everyday management."),
        ('What drives strain at work',
         ['Workload and unrealistic deadlines', 'Little control over how work is done', 'Unclear roles and poor relationships'],
         "Research on workplace stress points to a few common drivers. Workload that is too high or deadlines that are unrealistic. Little control over how or when work is done. Unclear roles, so people are unsure what is expected. Poor relationships or a lack of support from managers. Change that is badly communicated also adds strain. Notice that most of these are about the way work is organised, not about individuals. That is good news, because leaders can change work design. A well-being strategy that ignores these drivers will struggle to make a difference."),
        ('Linking well-being to workload',
         ['Look at the real data on workload', 'Ask where demands exceed capacity', 'Change the work, not only the person'],
         "Start with the data you already have. Look at overtime, absence, project loads and deadlines. Ask where demands regularly exceed capacity, and why. Then change the work, not only the person. That might mean removing low-value tasks, extending a deadline, adding resource, or clarifying priorities. Teaching resilience is useful, but it should not replace fixing a workload that is unsustainable. When people see leaders act on workload, it builds trust in the whole well-being effort, because it shows that the concern leads to a real change."),
        ('Measuring what matters',
         ['Choose a handful of measures', 'Combine data and pulse surveys', 'Track change over time'],
         "A strategy needs measures, or it becomes a list of good intentions. Choose a handful, not dozens. Combine hard data, such as absence, turnover and overtime, with short pulse surveys that ask people how they feel. Track the same measures over time, so that you can see whether things are improving. Share the results with the people involved, and be honest when the numbers are not good. Be careful not to use the data to blame managers. The point of measuring is to learn where to act, and to check that what you did made a difference."),
        ('Building a well-being plan',
         ['A clear owner and a small number of priorities', 'Actions with dates', 'A review every quarter'],
         "A good plan is short. It has a clear owner, a small number of priorities, and actions with dates. For example, reduce unplanned overtime in one department by a stated amount, or make sure every team has a regular conversation about workload. Link each priority to a measure, so you will know whether it worked. Review the plan every quarter. A plan that is written once and filed away has no effect. A plan that is visible and reviewed becomes part of how the organisation runs."),
        ('Policy into practice',
         ['Policies change nothing alone', 'Give managers training and prompts', 'Follow up on what happens'],
         "Policies are only words until managers apply them. A flexible working policy means little if managers quietly discourage it. To turn policy into practice, give managers training and simple prompts, such as a monthly question in one to ones about workload. Make it easy to find support, and easy to use. Then follow up on what actually happens, by asking whether managers are having these conversations, and what gets in the way. Leaders can help by doing it themselves, openly, since people copy what they see at the top."),
        ('Making support part of everyday management',
         ['Regular conversations, not crisis conversations', 'Visible signposting', 'Recognition of managers who do it well'],
         "The best support is built into everyday management, not reserved for a crisis. Encourage regular conversations about workload and energy in team meetings and one to ones. Make signposting visible, so that every employee knows what help exists and how to reach it. Recognise managers who handle these conversations well, since what gets recognised gets repeated. Over time, this shifts the culture, so that talking about well-being is normal, and asking for help is seen as sensible rather than as weakness."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Pick three measures to track', 'Name one workload change to make', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, choose three measures you will track, such as overtime, absence and a pulse score. Name one change to workload that you can make this quarter. Complete the workbook for this course, which has a plan template and a measures sheet. Then take the final quiz. When you reach the end of the last lesson you will receive your certificate and a personal review of how you worked through the course. A good strategy is practical and measured, so start small and review honestly."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('Work design', 'How tasks, workload, control and roles are organised for a team or person.'),
        ('Pulse survey', 'A short, frequent survey that tracks how people are feeling.'),
        ('Signposting', 'Making it clear what support exists and how to reach it.'),
        ('Well-being plan', 'A short list of priorities, owners, actions and measures reviewed regularly.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'Which part of work design causes the most strain in your area?',
        'What data on workload could you look at this month?',
        'What happens when a manager in your organisation raises a workload concern?',
    ],
    exercise=('One-page well-being plan', [
        'Write two priorities linked to workload.',
        'For each, name an owner, an action and a date.',
        'Choose one measure for each priority.',
        'Book the first quarterly review.',
    ]),
)
